  "sections": [
    {
      "phase": "reference",
      "title": "General Description",
      "items": [
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The C11 cuts an impressive figure parked alongside the Cherokees and Cessna's on a typical flight line.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The construction is a mixture of steel tubing and metal skinning for the forward fuselage, with fabric covering aft of the mainplane. The wings and tail surfaces are skinned in metal and the ailerons, rudder and elevator panels revert to fabric.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The Yak sits on a wide-tracked, inwardly-retracting undercarriage and a small, non-retracting tailwheel.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The brakes have been modified from the standard pneumatic drums to a set of hydraulic Cleveland discs which originated in a Cessna 421.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The Yak 11's 700 horse power, seven cylinder ASH 21 radial is hidden behind a tight fitting cowling with a cooling gill arrangement.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Access to the cockpit is easy — simply climb up the trailing edge of the wing and push in the knob at the front bottom corner of the forward or aft canopy.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Cyrillic script makes everything alien to a westerner, although CAA-approved labels take care of the important bits.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        }
      ],
      "criticality": "normal"
    },
    {
      "phase": "before_start",
      "title": "Canopy and Cockpit",
      "items": [
        {
          "type": "caution",
          "text": "Do not reach for the toggle above your head to close the canopy — all that will do is release the canopy's securing pins and allow the whole caboodle to fall off the back of the fuselage.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "action",
          "text": "Close canopy using the little knob at the forward corner and make sure that it is fully home before takeoff",
          "response": "FULLY HOME",
          "tickable": true,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The canopies are allowed to be opened in flight.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        }
      ],
      "criticality": "normal"
    },
